<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferrante I Gonzaga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1507">1507</a>–<a href="/years/1557">1557</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">House of Gonzaga</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferrante I Gonzaga was a son of Isabella d'Este and a leading general of the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, serving as Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and from 1546 as governor of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. In 1539 he bought the county of Guastalla from Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovica-torelli">Ludovica Torelli</a>, founding the Gonzaga line that ruled it, raised to a duchy under his grandson Ferrante II in 1621.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferrante-i-gonzaga.jpg" alt="Ferrante I Gonzaga" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fermo Guisoni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_di_Ferrante_I_Gonzaga_(Ghisoni).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pier Luigi tried to curb the local nobility, and on 10 September 1547 he was murdered in Piacenza by conspirators acting with the support of <b>Ferrante Gonzaga</b>, the imperial governor of Milan, whose troops at once occupied the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1539 Countess Ludovica Torelli sold Guastalla to <b>Ferrante Gonzaga</b>, general of Charles V and governor of Milan, whose branch of the Gonzaga ruled it from then on.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-guastalla">Duchy of Guastalla</a></li></ul></section>
